“The co-founders themselves came to the conclusion that they wished to depart OpenAI to start their own company, made this known to OpenAI’s leadership, and over several weeks negotiated an exit on mutually agreeable terms.” Anthropic accepted a $US4 billion investment from Amazon and another $US2 billion from Google two years later. The Reveal After OpenAI received another $US2 billion from Microsoft, Altman and another senior executive, Greg Brockman, visited Bill Gates at his sprawling mansion on the shores of Lake Washington, outside Seattle. The Microsoft founder was no longer involved in the company day to day but kept in regular touch with its executives.

Over dinner, Gates told them he doubted that large language models could work. He would stay sceptical, he said, until the technology performed a task that required critical thinking – passing an Advanced Placement biology test, for instance. Five months later, on August 24, 2022, Altman and Brockman returned and brought along an OpenAI researcher named Chelsea Voss.","entities":["How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence","Altman","Bill Gates","Lake Washington","visit to Bill Gates","Chelsea Voss","Advanced Placement biology test","Microsoft","large language models","The Age","Google","Anthropic","OpenAI","Greg Brockman","Seattle"],"facts":["How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> disinformation","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Musk","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Page","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Mark Zuckerberg","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Peter Thiel"]}
